{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/attempt-cap-and-retry-gate.md", "content": "# Attempt-cap handling and retry gate

In strict one-action puzzle interfaces, the protocol remains binding after the puzzle ends. When feedback says the instance is over—especially after exhausting the attempt cap—respond with exactly `{"action":"finish"}`. An empty assistant response or another `submit` is not a valid terminal action.

After a wrong submission, binary feedback gives no location information. Do not respond with a cosmetic or arbitrary variant (for example, changing only a suspected region's color). State the proposed model correction internally and test it against the complete demonstrations, including unchanged pairs and preserved cells. Only submit when the revised rule has evidence and reproduces all examples. If ambiguity remains and a demo is available, request one only to resolve a specific competing hypothesis; once demos are exhausted, further unsupported guesses compound cost and risk consuming the cap.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545261/attempts/dc2dcdf57b4240ea8dfdb9832237f673/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/attempt-cap-and-retry-gate.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/stripe-demo-sparse-transfer-review.md", "content": "# Stripe demos with a structurally different test

In the reviewed instance, two demonstrations used uniform horizontal stripes and transformed them into full-grid periodic horizontal color sequences. The test was also uniform stripes, but only 7×7 with a different ordering/repetition of colors. The demos revealed periodic output behavior and some palette/sequence roles, but a speculative transfer to the test was not validated against a second demo matching the test's scale or pattern. The chosen test sequence was therefore underdetermined: cycle order, phase, and stripe-role selection needed to be distinguished instead of guessed.

For similar cases, tabulate input and output stripe colors row-by-row, preserve all repeated and untouched stripes in the comparison, and test a candidate role-level sequence rule against every demo. Separate palette/cycle inference from phase and trigger inference. A small or differently structured test should not automatically receive a rasterized version of the large demo's periodic sequence. Ask for another pair only if its stripe arrangement can discriminate specific candidate rules; otherwise submit only the strongest supported extrapolation and retain the strict one-action JSON protocol.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545261/attempts/dc2dcdf57b4240ea8dfdb9832237f673/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/stripe-demo-sparse-transfer-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/periodic-pattern-transfer.md", "content": "# Periodic-pattern transfer from sparse marker grids

A sparse input may encode a palette/order and phase for a periodic output, rather than a local fill operation. Read the output demo as a 2D sequence: identify the minimal period, row-to-row phase shift, and top-left phase. Verify exact candidate mappings cellwise; common alternatives include `(r+c) mod p` and `(c-r) mod p`, which may look alike but diverge on asymmetric patterns.

Do not transfer a periodic sequence from one compelling demo unless the same role-level rule reproduces every example. No-op pairs and outputs with apparently different palettes/periods are evidence that the operation may be conditional or that the current abstraction is wrong. Following an incorrect submission, binary feedback cannot pinpoint phase versus palette errors. Avoid arbitrary variant cycling; request a discriminating demo while available, and if capped, make one best-supported choice rather than repeat rejected outputs.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545261/attempts/dc2dcdf57b4240ea8dfdb9832237f673/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/periodic-pattern-transfer.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/case-study-cross-motif-inference.md", "content": "# Cross-motif inference lesson

A recurring failure mode is guessing a motif's footprint or anchor from an incomplete visual impression. Before submitting, extract the exact colored motif from the input, list its non-background cells as offsets from a chosen anchor, and infer how the anchor corresponds to each scattered marker. Then apply the proposed rule to all markers in the demo and reproduce every output cell, including preserved cells and clipping at boundaries. Use demos to resolve orientation/anchor ambiguity rather than repeatedly submitting speculative grids. Distinct instances may share an abstract operation while using different motifs, so transfer the operation but relearn each motif's geometry and anchor from its own examples.

Also audit output dimensions and row lengths mechanically when possible; a syntactically plausible grid may fail the ARC grid schema if any row differs in length.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545261/attempts/dc2dcdf57b4240ea8dfdb9832237f673/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/case-study-cross-motif-inference.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/sparse-grid-demo-lesson.md", "content": "# Sparse-grid line completion lesson

In a sparse grid with scattered same-color marks, a demo may reveal that only specific aligned interior cells are painted a new color. The useful analysis is cellwise: record changed coordinates, separate changed background cells from original marks, then test row and column runs and their endpoints. Do not infer that every possible aligned pair is filled; verify every qualifying and non-qualifying run in the example. Transfer the smallest rule that exactly reproduces the full demo, preserving all original cells unless the output demonstrates replacement. In the reviewed instance, the test transformation was to paint background cells lying strictly between vertically aligned 0 markers using color 3, with marks and other cells preserved. First demo request gave no validation of this rule; the second demonstrated inserted 3s in line interiors. Exact output feedback confirmed the derived test answer.

Treat this as an evidence-backed pattern, not a universal ARC rule: obtain/compare examples to establish axis, endpoint, and color conditions before applying.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545261/attempts/dc2dcdf57b4240ea8dfdb9832237f673/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/sparse-grid-demo-lesson.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/diagonal-line-extrapolation.md", "content": "# Sparse motif: extrapolating a marked diagonal

A solved test contained a uniform background (8), two same-colored cells (2) at `(6,3)` and `(7,4)`, and nearby differently colored cells at `(7,5)` and `(8,5)`. The output extended only the color-2 diagonal backward with step `(-1,-1)` until the left grid boundary, adding 2s at `(5,2)`, `(4,1)`, and `(3,0)`. The original cells, including the other colors, remained unchanged.

Reusable inference: identify repeated same-color offsets in a small seed; test whether that vector defines a ray/line extended through background. Track direction and clipping against grid edges. Do not propagate unrelated adjacent colors or fill the entire diagonal without evidence. In this conversation, the demos were heterogeneous and mostly identity examples, so they did not establish the test's exact rule; the correct prediction came from recognizing the two-cell same-color diagonal and preserving all unrelated cells. This is a case note, not a universal transformation claim.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545261/attempts/dc2dcdf57b4240ea8dfdb9832237f673/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/diagonal-line-extrapolation.md"}
